of all the people involved in the horror  of Grenville tower there is one group at  the center of it all from whom we have  not yet heard the firefighters who were  actually in the building at the height  of the inferno one of them is Damien  McGee a London fireman for 20 years this  weekend is off to another firefighters  wedding with colleagues who were also in  Grenville tower he spoken exclusively to  Sky News about the horrors that  confronted it in the small hours of  Wednesday morning when we got there we  were coming over the Westway he could  see it miles in the distance and we were  hoping and praying that it was a  building site that it was a new build on  the construction that was going up  because it just how could it possibly be  we just couldn't believe what we were  saying no one on the truck everybody was  just in shock and we were miles and  miles away we realize what we've got  what we were heading into once they were  inside the tower the human cost became  clear I had no idea how many people who  got out by the stage how many people  were trapped well I can't tell you was  the screaming children screaming and  other residents in the block particular  I remember one kid's voice that I could  was sticking out higher-pitched and all  the other voices screaming screaming for  help and that's and I they probably had  some sort of hope when they say in all  the fire okay down there like you would  do for us to get in and help them you  know precedented temperatures employing  acrid smoke the crews battled to  exhaustion what I seen was when they  were coming downstairs they were nearly  on fire they were in bits or completely  exhausted and they were done in you mean  the temperatures are killing them and in  a lot of cases these crews work on  themselves without any firefighting  media now anyone who's seen the  television shows I've seen that that  whole building was completely engulfed  in flames we were all inside that  building and a lot of times the crews  that the ones that were there initially  in the crews ahead of me were upstairs  going into floors without any water  simply just looking around and just  tried trying to do their best banging on  doors and kicking in doors and trying to  get people out even those that  firefighters did manage to rescue  brought with them stomach-churning News  a little boy we'd a little boy who came  downstairs with him with his mother who  was in a very bad way and he seemed sort  of okay and very very shocked and car  lovely little fella probably got five  years old and we asked we need to know  what flat were you were in one time a  flat why you win is anyone else in that  flat because what's in it goes in there  and he's thrown I thought he said my  brother but he's dead his five year old  boy knew that his brother was dead yeah  he said yeah we asked was any Westerner  flies any when the family is anyone else  in the flats and he said just my brother  but he's dead now you're a 20-year serve  professional fire officer when a little  boy tells you that it breaks your heart  a little bit  it's very sad look I just you feel  useless and helpless we're there I know  that they looking at us to save them  everybody is you know we're hearing now  we'll save you but I mean it's not  saying it's unfair and others but I mean  we were put in a situation where we mean  people need to know where we can't you  know meanwhile that is I've got a high  the fire crews needed the help of police  to get in and out of the building as it  burned on I'd like to say a big massive  thank you to Metropolitan Police who did  an actually amazing job protecting us  from that pill thing that was falling  down on our heads and all around us and  so many of them were hit time and again  were massive lumps of debris and metal  and the composites and look to me like  concrete was falling off the top of the  building which it probably was and that  was bombing down all around as so many  people get smashed the piece of that  nevertheless running back and forwards  backwards and through that with  casualties and with people without their  selfless efforts many of those who did  survive would not have done so but for  some of the firefighters in the tower  there is a feeling of failure all the  firefighters that were there were gonna  be killing ourselves wishing that we  could have done more because of the  amount of people that we've lost and not  only that because we see some of them  but we watched families die well I mean  yeah I was I watched people die and I do  anything if I could go back I would  probably think well I'll do this but it  out of a difference you know I break the  rules more than what I would have done I  would have dropped a certain I would  have ran up there and I'd have done this  and that but yeah there's a few things  that I yeah that I wish that I would  probably donor could have done more but  essentially I mean that was a tyrant  many of the fire crews at the scene as  the blaze tore through the building  still do not feel comfortable speaking  about it what they saw will stay with  them for the rest of their lives  Debbie Bowden Sky News
 

